page_context[1]:
  - title: "linux-runink: the Runink River kernel"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/river/kernel/"
    date: "0001-01-01T00:00:00Z"
    category: "page"
    description: "linux-runink is the kernel of Runink River: the zen kernel, tuned for long data jobs instead of desktop response, with extra hardening, built for x86-64-v3 and packaged for Arch Linux in the AUR."
    content: "linux-runink is the kernel of Runink River, the open-source workstation operating system from Runink. It starts from the zen kernel, the same source that Arch Linux builds linux-zen from, and changes three things: how it shares the processor, how much it trusts itself, and which processors it is built for. It is also packaged for Arch Linux, as a community package in the AUR.\nHow it differs from linux-zen Tuned for long data jobs, not for desktop response zen tunes the kernel so a desktop answers fast. Runink River runs data pipelines and analytics, where the long job matters more than the next click. So linux-runink turns zen\u0026rsquo;s desktop tuning off and sets these defaults instead:\nTimer at 250 Hz instead of 1000 Hz, the kernel\u0026rsquo;s own suggested value for servers. Lazy preemption instead of full preemption, so a busy job is interrupted less often. You can still boot with preempt=full. Huge memory pages on request (madvise) instead of always. A database or JVM that asks for huge pages gets them, and other programs avoid the stalls. BBR congestion control and the fq queue by default, for steady network transfers under load. No automatic grouping by login session, so nice works across sessions, and per-CPU work queues for better cache use. Each of these can still be changed at boot or at run time. The reasons, with sources, are in the kernel notes.\nExtra hardening On top of what linux-zen already does, linux-runink:\nclears memory when it is freed, not only when it is handed out; keeps kernel memory caches apart instead of merging them; stops on detected data corruption instead of carrying on; starts the lockdown security module early in boot; leaves out /dev/mem and /proc/kcore, which expose memory directly; leaves out kexec, crash dumps and hibernation, so no other kernel can be loaded without a reboot and no memory image is written to disk. Built for x86-64-v3 The kernel is compiled for x86-64-v3 processors, the level that adds AVX2, BMI2, FMA and MOVBE to the 64-bit x86 baseline. The build stops on a processor without v3, so it never makes a kernel that cannot boot there. Set RUNINK_MARCH=x86-64 before the build to make a kernel for any 64-bit x86 processor.\nThe build checks its own settings Every setting above is listed in a file named config.require. The build reads the finished configuration and stops if any line is wrong. A new kernel version cannot drop one of these settings without someone noticing.\nInstall it on Arch Linux linux-runink is in the AUR. It is a community package: Arch Linux does not make or endorse it. It builds from source on your machine.\n1 2 3 4 git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/linux-runink.git cd linux-runink gpg --import keys/pgp/*.asc makepkg -si The gpg step imports the public keys of the kernel and zen maintainers, so the build can check their signatures on the source. The package installs next to your current kernel and adds its own boot entry through mkinitcpio. Add linux-runink-headers if you build out-of-tree modules.\nWhere the source lives Everything is public in the Runink River repository:\nbuild/pkgbuilds/runink-kernel/ holds the kernel package used for Runink River and the three configuration files: config.base (the zen configuration), config.delta (the changes above) and config.require (the checks). packaging/aur/linux-runink/ holds the AUR package. docs/KERNEL.md explains each setting. The kernel is GPL-2.0-only, like Linux. The AUR package downloads the kernel.org source and the zen patch, checks both signatures, and takes the configuration files from a signed release tag of the repository, each pinned by checksum.\n"
